---
# What actually keeps the B3 rehearsal copy from acting on the world — and it is not what the
# first attempt assumed.
#
# THE STORY, because the conclusion is worthless without it. This copy runs the LIVE sonarr
# database, so it knows the real download client and the real indexers. The plan was to fence it
# with a deny-all-egress NetworkPolicy. Measured 2026-08-07: that policy selected the pod
# correctly and did NOT block anything — a pod carrying the fence label reached daniel-server:443
# with the policy in force. (The same cluster's INGRESS policy, n8n-broker, is verifiably
# enforced, which makes the asymmetry a trap for the next person who reaches for egress.)
#
# WHAT DOES HOLD is the name-resolution boundary. Every outbound target in sonarr's database is a
# DOCKER NETWORK NAME, not an IP:
#
#     download client   qBittorrent   wireguard:8080
#     indexers (x7)     via Prowlarr  http://prowlarr:9696/...
#
# None of those resolve in the cluster, so the copy cannot reach them however hard it tries —
# confirmed by sonarr's own log: "Unable to retrieve queue and history items from qBittorrent".
#
# THIS PROBE ASSERTS THAT BOUNDARY, rather than trusting it. It is not a formality: B4-B7 bring
# prowlarr and qbittorrent INTO this cluster under exactly these Service names, and on that day
# the names start resolving. A still-rehearsing sonarr would then find them. This Job is what
# turns that from a silent change into a failed deploy.
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: sonarr-isolation-probe
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  activeDeadlineSeconds: 120
  backoffLimit: 0
  # Backstop cleanup: the deploy task always deletes this Job before re-applying it, but a day's
  # TTL means a completed Job doesn't sit around indefinitely if that step is ever skipped.
  ttlSecondsAfterFinished: 86400
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: sonarr-isolation-probe
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      enableServiceLinks: false
      containers:
        - name: probe
          image: {{ sonarr_k8s_probe_image }}
          command:
            - sh
            - -c
            - |
              # `getent hosts`, NOT nslookup. busybox's nslookup ignores the `search` list in
              # /etc/resolv.conf, so a BARE Service name never resolves through it — which made
              # the first version of this probe report every host "isolated" unconditionally,
              # including one that demonstrably existed. getent goes through musl's resolver,
              # which honours the search path, so it answers the question sonarr's own HTTP
              # client asks.
              #
              # The control is a bare Service name in this namespace, resolved the same way and
              # over the same single search-domain hop as the hosts below. A control using an
              # FQDN would not have caught the nslookup bug at all.
              if ! getent hosts sonarr >/dev/null 2>&1; then
                echo "CONTROL FAILED: the bare name 'sonarr' does not resolve in {{ k8s_namespace }},"
                echo "so short-name lookup is broken in this pod and every result below would say"
                echo "'isolated' regardless. This probe proves nothing."
                exit 1
              fi
              echo "control ok: bare Service names resolve in this namespace"
              RC=0
              for h in {{ sonarr_k8s_isolation_hosts | join(' ') }}; do
                if getent hosts "$h" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
                  echo "ISOLATION BROKEN: '$h' now resolves in {{ k8s_namespace }}."
                  echo "  The rehearsal sonarr can reach its real download client or indexers,"
                  echo "  and it holds the live database. Either cut it over (set"
                  echo "  sonarr_k8s_rehearsal=false) or stop it — do not leave it running."
                  RC=1
                else
                  echo "isolated: '$h' does not resolve"
                fi
              done
              exit $RC
          securityContext:
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
            capabilities:
              drop: ["ALL"]
          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: 10m
              memory: 16Mi
            limits:
              cpu: 200m
              memory: 64Mi
